= Open-source intelligence — Simple =

'''Open-source intelligence''' (OSINT) is intelligence created from information that is publicly or commercially accessible.

It is not just “searching the Internet.”

Professional OSINT starts with a defined question, collects relevant open information, checks where it came from, compares independent evidence, and produces an assessment for a specific purpose.

A U.S. statutory definition describes OSINT as intelligence produced from publicly available information and collected, exploited, and disseminated to answer a specific intelligence requirement.<ref name="uscode">[https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-136/t50/s3038/ U.S. Code, Department of Defense Strategy for Open-source Intelligence note.]</ref>

== Public information versus OSINT ==

A public news article, map, database, photograph, or social-media post is a source.

It becomes part of OSINT only after it is used in a structured intelligence process.

For example:

<pre>
public photograph
→ verify location
→ verify date
→ compare with other evidence
→ assess what happened
→ report confidence
</pre>

The intelligence product is the analysis, not the photograph by itself.

== Open does not always mean free ==

Some useful information is available only through paid subscriptions or commercial databases.

“Open source” in OSINT means the information can be obtained through lawful public or commercial channels.

It does not necessarily mean zero cost.

== Provenance matters ==

A reliable investigation records where evidence came from.

Useful details include:

* original URL;
* uploader or publisher;
* upload date;
* collection date;
* archived copy;
* file hash;
* notes about any changes.

A screenshot without source information is weaker than a preserved original with provenance.

== Independent corroboration ==

Several reports can look independent while actually copying the same original source.

If five websites repeat one false post, that is not five independent confirmations.

Investigators should trace claims toward the earliest available evidence and identify shared origins.

== Geolocation and time ==

Images and videos can sometimes be verified by matching:

* roads;
* buildings;
* terrain;
* signs;
* satellite imagery;
* shadows;
* weather.

An upload timestamp is not always the time the event happened.

Old images are often reposted as new events.

== Social media can mislead ==

Social-media content can be:

* fake;
* edited;
* miscaptioned;
* automated;
* coordinated;
* removed from context.

A viral post is not automatically credible.

Follower count and popularity are not evidence of truth.

== Confidence should be explicit ==

A good intelligence report separates:

* what was directly observed;
* what was inferred;
* what was assumed;
* how confident the analyst is.

Example:

<pre>
Observed: three vehicles appear in the image.
Inferred: they are probably military logistics vehicles.
Assumed: the image date is correct.
Confidence: moderate.
</pre>

This keeps inference from being mistaken for fact.

== Digital open-source investigations ==

Journalists, human-rights investigators, lawyers, and researchers also use open-source methods.

The Berkeley Protocol provides standards for collecting, preserving, verifying, and analyzing digital open-source information in investigations of international crimes and human-rights violations.<ref name="berkeley">[https://humanrights.berkeley.edu/publications/berkeley-protocol-on-digital-open-source-investigations/ Human Rights Center and OHCHR, ''Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations''.]</ref>

It emphasizes accuracy, legality, safety, privacy, and evidence preservation.

== Public information can still be sensitive ==

Information being public does not mean it is harmless to collect or republish.

Publishing someone's exact location or identity can create danger.

Investigators should collect only what is necessary and avoid exposing private people unnecessarily.

== Investigator safety ==

OSINT work can expose researchers to phishing, malware, harassment, and graphic material.

Professional teams may use isolated research environments, controlled accounts, secure storage, and procedures for handling disturbing content.

== Artificial intelligence ==

AI can help with translation, search, image comparison, summarization, and organizing large collections.

It can also invent facts or combine sources incorrectly.

AI-generated results should therefore be traced back to original evidence.

== OSINT is not open-source software ==

[[Open-source software]] is about software licenses and source-code rights.

OSINT is about producing intelligence from accessible information.

The two uses of “open source” are unrelated.

